This is the MPLIB library. It provides functions to calculate the unary
(self-energy) and binary (pairwise) electrostatic potentials arising from
image forces on ions confined in metallic nano-pores, hence the name.

Two pore geometries are supported: a slit (2D) metallic pore and a
cylindrical (1D) metallic pore. Both a numerically exact and a faster
polynomial-approximation variant are provided for each function; see the
per-function references below for the valid range of the approximations.

mplib is used by the sibling projects `single-file` (analytical solver for
ions in a single-file cylindrical pore) and `mpore-gcmc` (GCMC simulation of
ions in cylindrical/slit pores) via the Python ctypes bindings in
python/mplib_ctypes.py.

Build prerequisites
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- A C compiler and GNU Autotools (only needed if building from a git
  checkout rather than a release tarball; run `autoreconf -fi` first).
- GSL (GNU Scientific Library) -- required. `./configure` will fail with
  "GSL not found" if `gsl-config` isn't on your PATH.
- Python >= 3.4 -- required for the Python bindings (python/mplib_ctypes.py).
- A Fortran compiler (gfortran) -- optional. Only needed to build
  tests/fortran/u1.F, a smoke test for the Fortran-callable wrappers in
  lib/potentials_f.c (used historically to call mplib from Towhee). If no
  Fortran compiler is found, `./configure` prints a warning and skips that
  one test directory; the C library and Python bindings are unaffected.
- pytest -- needed to run the primary test suite (`make check`; see
  tests/python/test_mplib.py). `pip install pytest` if not already present.

Build and test
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./configure
    make
    make check     # builds+runs the C smoke tests and the pytest suite
    make install

The Python bindings (python/mplib_ctypes.py) load the compiled library via
a bare `ctypes.CDLL("libmplib.so")`, which relies on the OS dynamic
loader's default search path. After `make install`, make sure the install
prefix's lib/ directory is on LD_LIBRARY_PATH (or run `ldconfig` if
installing to a standard system location) before importing it from Python.

Configure options
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

--disable-assertions: disable all MPLIB assertions. Use only after the
programs are tested and run OK -- with assertions disabled, an
out-of-range input is undefined behaviour rather than a clean abort.

--enable-optimize: more aggressive optimization (-O3, no -g). Not
recommended while testing.

Functions provided
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. Ions confined in a slit (2D) metallic pore, to be used in MC
simulations (and actually used by towheemp):

    U1 = mplib_potential_unary (z, L)
    U2 = mplib_potential_binary (z1, z2, R, L)

with mplib_potential_binary_approx for a faster approximate evaluation.
See S Kondrat & A A Kornyshev, "Superionic state in double-layer
capacitors with nanoporous electrodes", J Phys: Condens. Matter 23,
022201 (2011), doi:10.1088/0953-8984/23/2/022201.

2. Ions confined in a cylindrical (1D) metallic pore:

    U1 = mplib_cylinder_u1 (r1, R)
    U2 = mplib_cylinder_u2 (rho1, rho2, phi, z, R)

with polynomial-approximation variants mplib_cylinder_u1_approx /
mplib_cylinder_u2_approx for faster (but less accurate -- see the source
for the valid range) evaluation. See:
  A A Lee, S Kondrat & A A Kornyshev, "Single-File Charge Storage in
  Conducting Nanopores", Phys. Rev. Lett. 113, 048701 (2014),
  doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.113.048701.

  C C Rochester, A A Lee, G Pruessner & A A Kornyshev, "Interionic
  Interactions in Conducting Nanoconfinement", ChemPhysChem 14(18),
  4121-4125 (2013), doi:10.1002/cphc.201300834.

Note: earlier internal versions of this library also included DFT/DDFT
potential functions (mplib_dft_u1/u2, mplib_ddft_W/Wx) used for an
unpublished project and for the dynamical-charging work in J. Phys. Chem.
C (2013). These have been removed from this public release, since they
were undocumented in-repo beyond "see notes elsewhere" (notes that don't
actually exist here) and aren't used by any of the currently-published
sibling projects. Get in touch if you need them.
